#!/usr/bin/env python3
# Generated by OrchestKit Claude Plugin
# Created: 2026-09-04
"""
gate.py

The plugin's single deterministic gate dispatcher. Design spec:
docs/audits/2026-09-04-determinism-audit.md, sections 3.1, 3.2, 5.

WHY this exists: the 2026-09-04 determinism audit found that of eleven
deterministic guard scripts referenced across this plugin, only ONE was
invoked from executable code -- the other ten fired only because a
`SKILL.md` prose sentence told a model to run them, which by the audit's
own test ("could an agent's judgment change this? If yes, it is not a
gate") means they were not gates at all. `gate.py` is the typed entry
point four of those checks now go through, so a `Stop` hook
(`src/hooks/src/lifecycle/gate-enforce.ts`, owned separately) can enforce
them from a ledger instead of trusting that a model chose to run them.

Exit codes are the contract (ACI-03) -- a caller must NEVER have to parse
prose to tell these apart:
  0 = gate PASSED
  1 = gate FAILED (the artifact is bad)
  2 = could not run (bad args, unreadable/malformed input, missing
      dependency -- e.g. the `jsonschema` package for `changes-schema`)

Subcommands:
  argument-graph <outline.json>
      Replaces the "Checklist -- all four must pass" at
      src/skills/write/SKILL.md:314-322 in full:
        - orphan (no orphan sections/units): every node's claim must trace
          back to the thesis via `thesis_link` -- either the node has its
          own directly, or it `depends_on` a node that (transitively)
          does. This is a chain walk, NOT "does this node have its own
          thesis_link" -- a node with none of its own is valid as long as
          something reachable through its depends_on chain has one.
        - cycle (no circular depends_on references): white/grey/black DFS
          over `depends_on` -- no node may depend, directly or
          transitively, on itself.
        - empty-evidence (no empty evidence): every node's `evidence`
          array must be non-empty.
        - ordering (dependency ordering): the flattened outline sequence
          (the Step 6 writing order) must respect `depends_on` -- a node
          cannot appear before every node listed in its `depends_on`.
          Positional over the SAME flattened index a cycle-free graph can
          still get wrong -- distinct from cycle detection, and tagged
          with its own `check` value so the two stay distinguishable in
          the output even though a genuine cycle usually also produces at
          least one ordering violation (one direction of a cycle's edges
          necessarily points to a later position in a strict list).
      Also reports `dangling-dependency` (a `depends_on` id with no
      matching node) as a fifth, practical check the prose assumes true
      by construction but this script cannot.
      All five run over the outline flattened to writer-unit nodes exactly
      as src/workflows/write-sections.js's `flattenUnits()` does: a
      section with no nested `units` array IS itself a node; a section
      WITH a `units` array contributes only its units, never itself.

  coverage <deep-read.json>
      Replaces the "Coverage Pre-Check" at src/agents/architect.md:28-41.
      Every `chapter_coverage` entry has a `status` in the allowed set
      (skipped-irrelevant entries must also carry a non-empty `reason`,
      per architect.md's own parenthetical); chapter numbers per book form
      a contiguous 1..max range; every `evidence_ids` reference resolves
      against the deep-read payload's `evidence` collection (a list of
      `{"id": ...}` records, or a dict keyed by id -- deep-reader.md does
      not pin one shape down for this file, so both are accepted). If
      `chapter_coverage` is absent entirely, the check is a no-op PASS --
      matching architect.md:41 ("If chapter_coverage is absent ... proceed
      ... as normal").

  citation-floor <article.md> --sources <sources.json>
      New gate, implements VER-18: the per-paragraph auditor is a
      PRESENCE check -- it verifies that claims which ARE cited are cited
      correctly, and is structurally blind to a paragraph that cites
      nothing. This gate counts citation occurrences in the article and
      classifies each as PRIMARY (a source quoted/referenced inline --
      biblical/Talmudic style refs, or any citation this gate cannot
      positively match to a known secondary-scholarship author) or
      SECONDARY (a citation this gate CAN positively match, via
      `sources.json`'s `author` field, to a piece of scholarship). Only
      secondary citations count toward the floors in
      thresholds.json > citationFloor -- a raw count would read healthy on
      an article that quotes primary texts constantly but engages no
      scholarship at all, which is exactly the blind spot this gate closes.
      Span detection REUSES citation-guard.py's `FOOTNOTE_RE` and
      `PAREN_CITATION_RE` (loaded dynamically -- see `_load_citation_guard`
      below, since a hyphenated filename can't be `import`ed directly)
      rather than reinventing citation-token regexes. Classification (which
      matches are "secondary") is this script's own logic on top of those
      spans, driven by `sources.json`.

  changes-schema <changes.json>
      Validates against the canonical on-disk
      src/skills/proofread/references/changes-schema.json (Draft 2020-12,
      via the `jsonschema` package already in requirements-dev.txt) --
      closing the "two copies of one contract, free to drift" risk the
      audit found in proofread.js/write-sections.js's inline schema
      duplicates (this script reads the file fresh every run; it never
      inlines a copy).

`--json` prints one JSON object to stdout:
    {"gate": "<name>", "status": "pass"|"fail"|"error", "detail": "<one
     line>", "violations": [...]}
Without `--json`, a short human-readable summary is printed instead. Both
are silent-ish on pass (one line).

Ledger side effect: after running, if a ledger path resolves AND its
parent directory already exists, the verdict is merged into it under
`gates.<gate-name>` (status/at/detail/violations), preserving every other
pre-existing key, written atomically (temp file + os.replace). Ledger path
resolution: `--ledger` if given; else
`$CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR/.academic-helper/session-state.json`; else
`./.academic-helper/session-state.json`. Per this project's anti-pattern
convention (CLAUDE.md: "Don't create `.academic-helper/` outside the setup
or init skills"), this script NEVER creates that directory -- if the
resolved ledger's parent directory does not already exist, the ledger
write is skipped silently and the process still exits with the gate's real
verdict code. A ledger-write failure of any other kind (corrupt existing
JSON, a permissions error, whatever) is likewise swallowed -- it is a side
effect, never the verdict, and must never change the exit code.

No third-party dependencies except `jsonschema` (already in
requirements-dev.txt), and that import is lazy -- confined to the
`changes-schema` subcommand -- so the other three subcommands work even in
an environment where it is not installed.
"""

